\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Conceptual comparison of the inter-community representation. (a) In the full network, infection can cross communities only along explicit channels incident to bridge nodes (thick black rings). (b) In the two-scale simulator, each community remains a local Gillespie/CTMC engine, while the macro interface uses community summaries and the aggregate weight $W_{ij}$ to generate an import rate and target placement. (c) Under uniform placement, the ideal two-scale process is equivalent to the surrogate network of Proposition~\ref {prop:surrogate}, in which every ordered cross-community node pair receives the weak rate $\beta _{\mathrm  {out}}W_{ij}/(N_iN_j)$. Orange denotes infected nodes and blue susceptible nodes.}}{7}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:schematic}{{1}{7}{Aggregate interface}{figure.1}{}}
